==============================================================================
SUMMARY -- THE CONJECTURE IS FALSE
==============================================================================
Clause (3) FAILS.  84 of 178 instances admit NO (E,e) on the 1,280-point grid
with dMEASURE >= 0; 22 of the 25 printed above still fail on a 13,984-point
grid (directions |dx|,|dy| <= 12, |e| from 1e-6 to 50, both signs); and three
of them fail on a 37,248-point sweep of the DISPLACEMENT plane d = e*E at
resolution 1/8 over a radius-12 box, plus 16,640 more at resolution 1/32 near
the apex.  In the perturbation regime the conjecture fails on 138 of 178.

WHY, exactly.  A vertex-vertex coincidence P[i] = Q[j] contributes exactly
FOUR entries to MEASURE and no crossing:

    segDeg  (i-1, j), (i, j)     Q[j] is an endpoint of both P-sides at P[i]
    segDegP (j-1, i), (j, i)     P[i] is an endpoint of both Q-sides at Q[j]

and no more than four, because a fifth would be Q touching its own boundary.
The move destroys all four.  The only local repayment is new proper crossings
between the two moved P-sides and the two Q-sides at the apex.  By the cone
criterion, the moved P-side through a_k crosses the Q-side through b_l iff

    sign cross(a_k, E) = sign cross(b_l, E) = sign cross(a_k, b_l) != 0,

so four crossings require cross(a_k,b_l) to have the SAME sign for all four
(k,l) -- i.e. the two local V's do NOT interleave.  Whenever the two
boundaries genuinely CROSS at the coincidence, the signs are mixed, the budget
is 1 or 2, and MEASURE must fall by 2 or 3.  Measured budget over the 178
instances: 1 (22x), 2 (97x), 3 (33x), 4 (26x); every small-e success had
budget 4.

MEASURE is a lossy over-count exactly at a coincidence -- one intersection
POINT is charged four times -- so nondegenerating cannot preserve it.  What is
consistent with all 178 instances is the weaker, slack-carrying statement

    MEASURE + 3 * |coincSet|   is non-increasing,

since the best admissible move never cost more than 3 (min over instances of
max over admissible moves of dMEASURE = -3, in BOTH the small-e and the full
grid).  That is a data-supported conjecture from 178 instances, not a theorem.
